<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
    <article xml:id="midterm2">
        <section xml:id="sec-mid2">
            <title>Midterm 2</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Below you will find a practice set (with solutions) and a sample exam. 
                </p>
            </introduction>
        
            <worksheet xml:id="midterm2-practice" margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
            <title>Midterm 2 Practice Set</title>
            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            For each set of criteria, sketch <em>one</em> graph that meets all criteria.
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}f(x) = 2</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(0) = 1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(2) = -2</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2^+}f(x) = -2</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2^-}f(x) = 0</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}f(x)</m> DNE
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(0) = 1</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}f(x) =1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(0) = 1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f'(0)&#x3C;0</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\dfrac{f(0+1) - f(0)}{1}>0</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(0+h)- f(0)}{h}> \frac{f(2) - f(0)}{2-0}</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f'(0+h) - f'(0)}{h}&#x3C;0</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Consider the function <m>g(t)=t^{2}+3.</m>
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Find the average rate of change in <m>g(t)</m> between time <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=2.5.</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Find the average rate of change of <m>g(t)</m> between time <m>t=2</m> and time <m>t=2+h</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Find the instantaneous rate of change of <m>g(t)</m> at <m>t=2</m> using your answer from part (b) and the limit definition of the derivative.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the derivative functions of the following functions:
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>s(x) = e^{-3x+1}+ 5\ln(3x)</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>p(t) = e^{t}\cos(t)</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>h(z) = \dfrac{z}{1+\ln(z^2)}</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>j(x)=dx +\frac{x^{2}}{d}</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\ell(x) = f(x^{3} -1) \cdot k(x)</m>, where <m>f</m> and <m>k</m> are unknown functions
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </page>
            
            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
            For the function <m>y</m> below, label
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            One critical point of <m>y</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y'</m> is positive.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y'</m> is negative.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y''</m> is positive.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y''</m> is negative.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One inflection point of <m>y</m>.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
        
                        </p>
                        <image source="graphwithlabels.png" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>Graph of y as a function of x.</shortdescription>
        </image>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Find the linear approximation of <m>e^{x}</m> centered at <m>a = 1</m>.
            Denote this <m>L(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Find the quadratic approximation of <m>e^{x}</m> centered at <m>a = 1</m>.
            Denote this <m>Q(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Evaluate <m>L(1.1)</m> and <m>Q(1.1)</m>.
            These two values give approximations of what number?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Do you expect <m>L(1.1)</m> to be an overestimate or an underestimate of the value it is approximating? Sketch a graph to justify your answer.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Do you expect <m>L(1.1)</m> or <m>Q(1.1)</m> to be a better approximation?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </page>

            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            You know that a function <m>k(x)</m> satisfies the following criteria:
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>k(2) =3</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>k'(2) = -1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>k''(x) &#x3C; 0</m> for all <m>x</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>

<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            You have enough information to write the linear approximation <m>L(x)</m> for <m>k(x)</m> centered at which <m>x</m>-value? Write the equation of <m>L(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Is it more appropriate to use <m>L(x)</m> to estimate <m>k(-2.25)</m> or <m>k(2.25)</m>? Why?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Use <m>L(x)</m> to estimate the value that you chose above.
            Is your estimate an overestimate or underestimate of the true value?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </page>
            <page>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                           <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Draw an example of an updating function <m>f(m_{t})</m> with a stable equilibrium at <m>m_{t} = 2</m> such that the cobwebbing algorithm oscillates on either side of the equilibrium.
            Give a rough estimate of <m>f'(2).</m> What does the Stability Theorem say about the equilibrium?
            <image source="diagonal.png" width="50%">
            </image>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Draw an example of an updating function <m>f(m_{t})</m> with an unstable equilibrium at <m>m_{t} = 2</m> such that the cobwebbing algorithm oscillates on either side of the equilibrium.
            Give a rough estimate of <m>f'(2).</m> What does the Stability Theorem say about the equilibrium?
            <image source="diagonal.png" width="50%">
            </image>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Draw an example of an updating function <m>f(m_{t})</m> with a half-stable equilibrium at <m>m_{t} = 2.</m> Give a rough estimate of <m>f'(2).</m> What does the Stability Theorem say about the equilibrium?
            <image source="diagonal.png" width="50%">
            </image>
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </page>

            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Consider the modified logistic dynamical system <m>z_{t+1}=rz_{t}(3-z_{t})</m>.
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Determine all of the positive equilibrium points of this system.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            For each positive equilibrium point, determine the values of <m>r</m> which make that point stable using the Stability Theorem.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of <m>g(x) = \sqrt{x}-x</m> on the domain <m>[0,2]</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the critical number(s) of <m>f(x) = 1 + 2x^{2} + x^{\frac{1}{3}}</m> and classify them as being local minima, local maxima, or neither of the function <m>f(x)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Use the graph of <m>\mathbf{h'(x)}</m> (the <em>derivative</em> of <m>h(x)</m>) to answer the questions.
                            <image source="h'.png" width="50%">
                                <shortdescription>Graph of h prime, which has a zero at x=-1 and x=3. The 
                                    graph increases until x=0.25, decreases until x=3, then increases for x bigger than 3.
                                </shortdescription>
                            </image>
                            <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            What are the critical number(s) of <m>h(x)</m>?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Classify each critical number as a local maximum, local minimum, or neither of <m>h(x)</m> using the first derivative test.
            Illustrate your reasoning with a first derivative sign chart.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            What does the second derivative test say about each critical number?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </page>

            </worksheet>


            <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
            <title>Midterm 2 Practice Set (with solutions)</title>

            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            For each set of criteria, sketch <em>one</em> graph that meets all criteria.
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}f(x) = 2</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(0) = 1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(2) = -2</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2^+}f(x) = -2</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2^-}f(x) = 0</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}f(x)</m> DNE
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(0) = 1</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}f(x) =1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f(0) = 1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>f'(0)&#x3C;0</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\dfrac{f(0+1) - f(0)}{1}>0</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(0+h)- f(0)}{h}> \frac{f(2) - f(0)}{2-0}</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f'(0+h) - f'(0)}{h}&#x3C;0</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Various answers are possible. Please see an instructor if you would like to check your work or discuss possible 
        solutions.
                        </p>
                    </solution>

                    
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Consider the function <m>g(t)=t^{2}+3.</m>
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Find the average rate of change in <m>g(t)</m> between time <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=2.5.</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Find the average rate of change of <m>g(t)</m> between time <m>t=2</m> and time <m>t=2+h</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Find the instantaneous rate of change of <m>g(t)</m> at <m>t=2</m> using your answer from part (b) and the limit definition of the derivative.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\frac{g(2.5)- g(2)}{2.5-2} = 4.5</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\frac{g(2+h)-g(2)}{h} = \frac{4h+h^2}{h}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>g'(2) = \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{g(2+h)-g(2)}{h}  =\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{4h+h^2}{h} = 4 </m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the derivative functions of the following functions:
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>s(x) = e^{-3x+1}+ 5\ln(3x)</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>p(t) = e^{t}\cos(t)</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>h(z) = \dfrac{z}{1+\ln(z^2)}</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>j(x)=dx +\frac{x^{2}}{d}</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\ell(x) = f(x^{3} -1) \cdot k(x)</m>, where <m>f</m> and <m>k</m> are unknown functions
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>s'(x) = -3e^{-3x+1} + \frac{5}{3x} \cdot 3</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>p'(t) = e^t \cos(t) -e^t\sin(t) </m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>h'(z) = \frac{1 + \ln(z^2)-2}{(1+\ln(z^2))^2}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>j'(x) = d + \frac{2}{d}x</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\ell'(x)= f'(x^3-1)\cdot 3x^2 \cdot k(x) + f(x^3-1)\cdot k'(x)</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>
            </page>
            
            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
            For the function <m>y</m> below, label
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            One critical point of <m>y</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y'</m> is positive.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y'</m> is negative.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y''</m> is positive.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One point where <m>y''</m> is negative.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            One inflection point of <m>y</m>.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
        
                        </p>
                        <image source="graphwithlabels.png" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>Graph of y as a function of x.</shortdescription>
        </image>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Various answers are possible. Please see an instructor if you would like to check your work or discuss possible 
        solutions.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Find the linear approximation of <m>e^{x}</m> centered at <m>a = 1</m>.
            Denote this <m>L(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Find the quadratic approximation of <m>e^{x}</m> centered at <m>a = 1</m>.
            Denote this <m>Q(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Evaluate <m>L(1.1)</m> and <m>Q(1.1)</m>.
            These two values give approximations of what number?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Do you expect <m>L(1.1)</m> to be an overestimate or an underestimate of the value it is approximating? Sketch a graph to justify your answer.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Do you expect <m>L(1.1)</m> or <m>Q(1.1)</m> to be a better approximation?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>L(x) = e + e(x-1)</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>Q(x) = e + e(x_1) + \frac{e}{2}(x-1)^2</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>e^{1.1} \approx L(1.1) = e +e(0.1) \approx 2.990</m>
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>e^{1.1} \approx Q(1.1) = e +e(0.1) +\frac{e}{2}(0.1)^2 \approx 3.004</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        We expect an underestimate, as seen on the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/hraenfigux">graph of <m>e^x</m> with its linear 
                                        approximation at <m>x=1</m></url>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>Q(1.1)</m>, since <m>Q(x)</m> also matches <m>e^x</m> in concavity at <m>x=1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>
            </page>

            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            You know that a function <m>k(x)</m> satisfies the following criteria:
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>k(2) =3</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>k'(2) = -1</m>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            <m>k''(x) &#x3C; 0</m> for all <m>x</m>
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>

<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            You have enough information to write the linear approximation <m>L(x)</m> for <m>k(x)</m> centered at which <m>x</m>-value? Write the equation of <m>L(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Is it more appropriate to use <m>L(x)</m> to estimate <m>k(-2.25)</m> or <m>k(2.25)</m>? Why?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Use <m>L(x)</m> to estimate the value that you chose above.
            Is your estimate an overestimate or underestimate of the true value?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The center is <m>x=2</m>, and <m>L(x) = 3 - (x-2) = -x+5</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>k(2.25)</m>, since <m>x=2.25</m> is closest to the center.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>k(2.25) \approx L(2.25) = 2.75</m>. This is an overestimate, since <m>k</m> is
                                        concave down.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>
            </page>
            <page>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                           <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Draw an example of an updating function <m>f(m_{t})</m> with a stable equilibrium at <m>m_{t} = 2</m> such that the cobwebbing algorithm oscillates on either side of the equilibrium.
            Give a rough estimate of <m>f'(2).</m> What does the Stability Theorem say about the equilibrium?
            <image source="diagonal.png" width="50%">
            </image>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Draw an example of an updating function <m>f(m_{t})</m> with an unstable equilibrium at <m>m_{t} = 2</m> such that the cobwebbing algorithm oscillates on either side of the equilibrium.
            Give a rough estimate of <m>f'(2).</m> What does the Stability Theorem say about the equilibrium?
            <image source="diagonal.png" width="50%">
            </image>
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Draw an example of an updating function <m>f(m_{t})</m> with a half-stable equilibrium at <m>m_{t} = 2.</m> Give a rough estimate of <m>f'(2).</m> What does the Stability Theorem say about the equilibrium?
            <image source="diagonal.png" width="50%">
            </image>
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Various answers are possible. Please see an instructor if you would like to check your work or discuss possible 
        solutions.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>
            </page>

            <page>
                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Consider the modified logistic dynamical system <m>z_{t+1}=rz_{t}(3-z_{t})</m>.
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Determine all of the positive equilibrium points of this system.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            For each positive equilibrium point, determine the values of <m>r</m> which make that point stable using the Stability Theorem.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Solve <m>z^*=rz^*(3-z^*)</m> to find <m>z^*= \frac{3r-1}{r}</m>, which is positive
                                        when <m>r \gt \frac{1}{3}</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f(x) = rx(3-x) = 3rx-rx^2</m>, so <m>f'(x) = er-2rx</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f'\left(\frac{3r-1}{r}\right) = 2-3r </m>, so <m>z^*</m> is stable when
                                        <m>-1 \lt 2-3r \lt 1</m>, or when <m>\frac{1}{3} \lt r \lt 1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of <m>g(x) = \sqrt{x}-x</m> on the domain <m>[0,2]</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            We find critical numbers of <m>g</m> by solving <m>g'(x) = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}} - 1 = 0</m> and also 
                            where <m>g'(x)</m> does not exist. <m>g'(x) = 0</m> when <m>x=0.25</m> and <m>g'(x)</m> does not exist 
                            when <m>x=0</m>, which are both in the domain we are considering.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <m>g(0) = 0</m>, <m>g(0.25)= 0.25</m>, and <m>g(2) = \sqrt{2}-2</m>. So the absolute maximum of 
                            <m>g</m> is <m>0.25</m> (and is achieved at <m>x=0.25</m>), and the absolute minimum is <m>sqrt{2}-2</m>
                            (and is achieved at <m>x=2</m>).
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the critical number(s) of <m>f(x) = 1 + 2x^{2} + x^{\frac{1}{3}}</m> and classify them as being local minima, local maxima, or neither of the function <m>f(x)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 4x+ \frac{1}{3}x^{-2/3}</m>, which is zero at <m>x= (-1/12)^{3/5}</m>, and does not exist at
                            <m>x=0</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Using the first derivative test, we can test three <m>x</m>-values: one less than <m>x= (-1/12)^{3/5}</m>,
                            one between <m>x= (-1/12)^{3/5}</m> and <m>x=0</m>, and one greater than <m>x=0</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            We find that <m>f'(-1) \lt 0</m> (so <m>f</m> is decreasing), <m>f'(-0.1) \gt 0</m> (so <m>f</m> is 
                            increasing), and <m>f'(1) \gt 0</m> (so <m>f</m> is increasing). This means <m>x= (-1/12)^{3/5}</m>
                            is the location of a local minimum of <m>f</m>, and <m>x=0</m> is neither.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>

                <exercise>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Use the graph of <m>\mathbf{h'(x)}</m> (the <em>derivative</em> of <m>h(x)</m>) to answer the questions.
                            <image source="h'.png" width="50%">
                                <shortdescription>Graph of h prime, which has a zero at x=-1 and x=3. The 
                                    graph increases until x=0.25, decreases until x=3, then increases for x bigger than 3.
                                </shortdescription>
                            </image>
                            <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            What are the critical number(s) of <m>h(x)</m>?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Classify each critical number as a local maximum, local minimum, or neither of <m>h(x)</m> using the first derivative test.
            Illustrate your reasoning with a first derivative sign chart.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            What does the second derivative test say about each critical number?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>x=-1,3</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>x=-1</m> is a local minimum, while <m>x=3</m> is neither. A first derivative sign chart 
                                        should change from negative to positive at <m>x=-1</m> (so the behavior of <m>f</m>
                                        is decreasing to increasing), and from positive to positive 
                                        at <m>x=3</m> (so the behavior of <m>f</m> is increasing to increasing).
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>h''(-1) \gt 0</m>, so the second derivative test says that <m>x=-1</m> is a local minimum.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>h''(3) = 0</m>, so the second derivative test is inconclusive.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </exercise>
            </page>

            

            </worksheet>

