                <page>
                    <exercise>
                        <statement>
                            <p>
                                Let <m>h(x)</m> be a function such that <m>h(-2) = -1</m> and <m>h'(-2) =3</m>.
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Write the equation for <m>L(x)</m>, the linear approximation of <m>h(x)</m> centered at <m>a=-2</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Use <m>L(x)</m> to approximate <m>h(-2.25)</m>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Suppose you also know that <m>h''(x) \lt 0</m> for all <m>x</m>.
            Sketch a rough graph of <m>h(x)</m> for <m>x</m>-values close to <m>-2</m>, including the graph of <m>L(x)</m>.
            Be sure to label both <m>h(x)</m> and <m>L(x)</m>.
            
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
